# shir_nodes/field_strip_end.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# The cursor advance behind a CASE-GUARDED front-chop:
#
#   case $s in
#     *"D"*) x=${s%%D*}; s=${s#*D} ;;   # hit  → head field + advance
#     *)     x=$s;        s=""      ;;   # miss → tail, then DRAIN
#   esac
#
# `FieldStrip` deliberately does NOT move the cursor on a miss (bash's
# `${s#*D}` is a no-op when the pattern does not match), but the guarded
# pair's fallback arm does something stronger: it EMPTIES `s`, so every
# later read of the field yields "". FieldStripEnd encodes exactly that:
# the ABSOLUTE byte offset just past the first `delim` at/after `off`,
# or the END of `text` when no `delim` follows it (drain). Draining is
# what makes dropping the guard sound — a grab from the end reads ""
# exactly like a grab of the emptied string, so the whole `case`
# collapses to the two cursor statements with no per-chop fnmatch over
# the row.
#
# The result is a decimal STRING (the same channel every shell scalar
# number travels), consumed by the companion FieldGrab reads of the same
# cursor var. Emitted by `str-chop-cursor` only for a recognized guard.
#
# Backends: C (strchr-at-offset + strlen-on-miss; target-gated offer —
#   other backends accept by adding a render arm and their target to
#   the gate, the FieldStrip offer pattern).
# Fallback: sh2.fieldStripEnd(text, off, delim)

node FieldStripEnd
tag "FieldStripEnd"
kind expr
field text: expr
field off: expr
field delim: string
spec: none (unstated)
